<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning from Mistakes – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn from This Experience?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain daily habits and routines. Interactive: Respond to classmates' presentations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain study habits and routines that support academic growth by sharing specific examples (e.g., "Every morning, I review my vocabulary, and in the evening, I write a summary of what I learned.").
            Can respond to classmates' presentations on learning from mistakes by asking and responding to questions, meeting the specific standard (e.g., "What did you learn from this experience?" "I learned to study harder.").
            Can ask and answer questions about helpful routines and habits in discussions, directly following the specific standard (e.g., "What helps you focus?" "Studying in a quiet room.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to quickly think about a small mistake they made recently (e.g., forgetting something, not understanding a task). Students turn to a partner and briefly share their mistake and one thing they learned from it. (e.g., "Last week, I forgot my homework. I learned to organize my materials better."). This activates prior knowledge and sets the theme for "Learning from Mistakes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reflecting on a personal academic mistake, using past simple for the mistake and future tense for improvement, and "should" for advice. For instance, "Last month, I *received* low <b>grades</b> on a science test because I *didn't study carefully* enough. I *learned* that I <b>should practice</b> every day. Next time, I *will study* more and ask for <b>feedback</b>." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending with /d/ (received, learned), /ɪd/ (wanted - if applicable), and /t/ (practiced). The teacher also highlights the pronunciation of /ð/ in "the" and /d/ in "did" for phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled reflection.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I get good grades on that test?" (Expected answer: "No.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn from that experience?" (Expected answer: "To study more carefully.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will I do differently next time?" (Expected answer: "You will study more.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two 8th-grade students discussing their recent struggles with a challenging project and how they plan to improve. The dialogue is authentic in register and focuses on the target vocabulary (<b>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, practice, improve, learn</b>) and grammar (past simple, future tense, 'should').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend and extract specific information about mistakes made and plans for future improvement from an authentic spoken interaction, preparing them to produce similar language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue twice.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the two students talking about?" (Expected answer: "Their project mistakes and how to do better.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements based on the dialogue. E.g., "The students got perfect <b>grades</b> on their project." (False). "One student *learned* to ask for <b>feedback</b>." (True). Students mark T/F and correct the false statements orally, explaining why using phrases from the dialogue.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using past simple to describe mistakes and what they *learned*, future tense to describe future <b>improvements</b>, and "should" for giving advice. Focus on the pronunciation of -ed verbs and distinguishing minimal pairs like /ð/ (they) and /d/ (day) in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a worksheet. Students complete them orally in pairs, using target vocabulary and grammar. (e.g., "I made a <b>mistake</b> when I didn't ____. I <b>learned</b> to ____." "To get <b>better grades</b>, I <b>should</b> ____.") The teacher circulates, providing immediate pronunciation feedback on -ed verb endings (/d/, /ɪd/, /t/) and minimal pairs if mispronounced (e.g., "Did you say 'day' or 'they' in that sentence?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Advice Chain Drill):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student shares a simple hypothetical academic problem (e.g., "I forgot my <b>homework</b>."). The next student responds with advice using "You <b>should</b>..." and then shares their own problem. (e.g., "You <b>should</b> write it down. My problem is I find <b>study</b>ing <b>challenging</b>."). The chain continues, encouraging natural use of "should" for advice. The teacher monitors for appropriate usage and clear pronunciation of target words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Q&A with Sentence Frames):</b> Students work in pairs. They use provided sentence frames to ask and answer questions about past learning experiences and future <b>goals</b>.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What <b>mistake</b> did you make recently, and what did you <b>learn</b>?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I *tried* to finish my project quickly, but I *made* many <b>mistakes</b>. I <b>learned</b> to *work* more <b>carefully</b>. Next time, I *will plan* my time <b>well</b>." (Teacher emphasizes /t/ in 'tried' and /d/ in 'learned', 'planned').</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "What <b>habits</b> <b>should</b> students have for <b>success</b>?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "They <b>should</b> <b>study</b> regularly and ask for <b>feedback</b>." (Teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /ð/ in 'they').</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-Play: "My Academic Challenge and My Plan for <b>Improvement</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student plays the role of a student who has experienced an academic challenge or made a <b>mistake</b> (e.g., received low <b>grades</b>, struggled with <b>homework</b>, found a subject <b>challenging</b>). The other student acts as a supportive classmate who asks questions, helps them reflect, and offers constructive <b>feedback</b> and advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Scenario Selection:</b> Each pair chooses one of the following scenarios to act out:
                            <ol type="a">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Getting a low grade on a test.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Struggling to finish a group project.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finding it hard to concentrate during online classes.</li>
                            </ol>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Role-Play (5-7 minutes):</b>
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (Problem): Explains the situation using past simple (e.g., "I *received* low <b>grades</b> on my math test last week because I *didn't practice* enough.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (Advisor): Asks clarifying questions (e.g., "What did you <b>learn</b> from this <b>experience</b>?", "What <b>habits</b> can you <b>improve</b>?"). Student B provides advice using "You <b>should</b>..." and helps Student A articulate future plans using the future tense (e.g., "You <b>should study</b> for 30 minutes every day.", "Next time, you <b>will try</b> to ask the teacher for help.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should aim to use a variety of the target vocabulary (<b>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, better, successful, challenging, carefully, well</b>) and focus on clear pronunciation, especially of -ed verbs.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role Switch:</b> Students switch roles and repeat the role-play with a different scenario or the same scenario from the other perspective.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the role-play from Stage 4. The teacher circulates, making notes on an observation checklist. Simultaneously, students use a simple peer assessment form to give constructive <b>feedback</b> to their partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fluency & Clarity:</b> Did students speak clearly and with reasonable fluency?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Grammar:</b> Did students correctly use past simple (for mistakes), future tense (for improvement), and "should" (for advice)? (e.g., "I *learned*", "I *will study*", "You *should practice*").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Did students use vocabulary related to the theme (e.g., <b>mistakes, grades, study, habits, feedback, improvement</b>)?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Was the pronunciation of -ed verbs (e.g., *practiced* /t/, *learned* /d/) and common words intelligible?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Did students interact effectively, asking and answering questions?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I <b>learned</b> today about talking about <b>mistakes</b> is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need to <b>practice</b> using ____ to give advice."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of explaining your <b>habits</b> or giving advice today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reflecting on <b>mistakes</b> help you achieve academic <b>success</b> in the future?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today's speaking focus allowed most students to confidently explain their routines and respond to classmates. The use of past simple and future tense for *improvement* was generally good. However, some students still struggled with the correct pronunciation of -ed endings, particularly distinguishing /d/ and /t/. I noticed a few confusions with minimal pairs like "think" and "sink" when asking for ideas. I will need to provide more targeted pronunciation practice and maybe a specific mini-activity on these sounds in the next lesson to reinforce listening for and producing these sounds <b>carefully</b>. The "should" for advice came naturally, but sometimes the advice lacked specificity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will apply the grammar and vocabulary learned today to write a short paragraph about a personal learning experience, including a <b>mistake</b> they *made* and how they *plan* to *improve* using helpful <b>habits</b>. This will allow students to consolidate the spoken language into written form, focusing on accuracy in sentence structure and further embedding the newly acquired vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">~7 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>